A||||2||ounmongk||Ugh, another headlight problem. ||||1||
Z||000000||ounmongk||08-03-2000||11:46 AM||||hey all, both of my headlights fail to come up, but only if the hood is closed... If I were to get out of the car and fully open the front compartment lid, the lights rise with no problem! The motors turn. I'm looking at these "headlight actuator rebuild kits" from Mr. Dickman... is this all I need? or should I look into other parts also? '87 SE ||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||08-03-2000||12:44 PM||||I suspect that you have one of two possible problems:<P>1)The rounded ears atop the headlight frame are digging into the rubs strips on the underside of the headlight doors in the hood.  Look at the rub strips - if they're grooved where the ears contact them, you may be looking at the problem.  <P>2)The headlight-raising mechanism is binding at some point.<P>The way Fiero headlight motors know when to stop, is when they "feel" the mechanism hit a physical stop.<P>In the case of the old-style (1986-earlier) headlight motors, the high torque switch (HTS) accomplishes   this function.  For your 1987 model, an electronic controller waits for the motor to stall out, then shuts the juice off.<P>Hope this helps you solve it   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  Good luck. <P>Rebuilding the headlight motors might help, if what's binding is inside the motor-gear case.  It's also a good idea anyway, if the motors haven't been properly rebuilt already.      <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 08-03-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-03-2000||07:20 PM||||if there is a groove worn on the underside of doors where that ear rubs, you can cut a strip of thin smooth metal and put it over the bad plastic with a little dab or silcone. take your finger after and wipe a little lithium grease on the surface. If thats your prob, itll work smoother than new.||||reg||1
